input.user_message:
{
  "narrative_body": "Margus Tsahkna, Estonia's foreign minister, flew to Hanoi this week as the first EU foreign minister to visit Vietnam since its recent leadership reshuffle — and he said plainly why: the American president's tariff pressures and criticism of European defence spending are pushing Europe to look for partners elsewhere.\n\nThe visit, which ran April 22–24 and brought 12 Estonian companies to a Ho Chi Minh City business seminar, deployed Estonia's digital governance expertise as the primary draw. Mr Tsahkna offered Vietnamese officials help with e-governance — bureaucracy reduction, transparency, cost savings — and positioned Estonia as a gateway for Vietnamese businesses into the EU single market. Vietnam's prime minister, Le Minh Hung, asked in return that Estonia lobby Brussels to ratify the EU-Vietnam Investment Protection Agreement and lift the European Commission's yellow card on Vietnamese seafood imports. Estonian diplomatic capital was being traded for trade access, and the exchange was real on both sides. Vietnam has maintained neutrality on Ukraine; Mr Tsahkna used the occasion to press Europe's view that Russia poses an existential threat, to an audience that has not said so. That Estonia is running this kind of messaging diplomacy in Southeast Asia, explicitly in response to transatlantic strain, marks a new geographic reach in an established pattern: a small country converting its digital brand into political currency.\n\nEven as he extended that reach outward, Mr Tsahkna was making a harder call at home. The same week, he joined Hanno Pevkur, the defence minister, military intelligence chief Colonel Ants Kiviselg, and parliamentary Foreign Affairs Committee chairman Marko Mihkelson in publicly contradicting Zelensky's claim that Russia may be preparing to invade the Baltic states. \"We don't see Russia concentrating its forces or preparing in any way militarily to attack NATO or the Baltic states,",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "US pauses ammunition deliveries to Estonia for the duration of the Iran conflict, defence minister seeks alternative procurement",
      "summary": "Defence Minister Hanno Pevkur disclosed after a phone call with US Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth that Washington has paused all ammunition deliveries to Estonia—including HIMARS rockets and Javelin anti-tank weapons—until at least the end of the Iran war, citing US operational requirements. Pevkur said Estonia may need to revisit prior procurement decisions if the pause extends, and indicated Estonia is seeking larger compensatory purchases from the US as well as exploring other suppliers; separately, a new €300 million Turkish-owned ammunition factory (ARCA) was announced for North-Kiviõli."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coop Pank reports profit growth for fourth consecutive quarter and enters investment services market",
      "summary": "Coop Pank posted a Q1 2026 net profit of €8.3 million, up 5% year-on-year and 15% quarter-on-quarter, maintaining higher capital efficiency than LHV; the bank announced its entry into investment services by hiring a former LHV executive to build a new business line, positioning itself in an increasingly competitive Estonian investment market. The bank also made supervisory board committee changes and disclosed share buyback activity."
    },
    {
      "headline": "President Karis makes official visit to Greece, co-opens Delphi Economic Forum",
      "summary": "President Alar Karis made a three-day official visit to Greece where he met President Konstantinos Tassoulas, Parliament speaker Nikitas Kaklamanis, and education minister Sofia Zacharaki; he and Tassoulas jointly opened the Delphi Economic Forum, where Karis argued Europe's competitiveness must be built on its own values rather than copying other economic models. Karis also visited the EU Cybersecurity Agency (ENISA) and called on Estonian-linked clergy and communities in Greece."
    }
  ]
}

